      "content": "Ah, my apologies—I got a little carried away with the goblin thing. Let me get back to the physics. The pitot tube’s open end faces directly into the airflow, so it captures the full impact pressure (dynamic + static). The static port, meanwhile, reads only the ambient atmospheric pressure. The difference between these two is the dynamic pressure, which follows Bernoulli’s equation: dynamic pressure = ½ × air density × airspeed². So the instrument measures that pressure difference and solves for airspeed, giving you indicated airspeed. That’s the core mechanism—no goblins involved, just fluid dynamics. But if you want a mental image, think of it as a tiny, very precise wind gauge, not a creature. Does that clear up the pressure differential part?",
