    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Blockage is a serious issue, yes. If the pitot tube’s ram air opening gets clogged—by insects, ice, or debris—the dynamic pressure can’t enter, so the airspeed indicator will read zero or freeze at the last value, depending on whether the drain hole is also blocked. If only the static port is blocked, the airspeed will read incorrectly, often showing a false increase during climbs and a false decrease during descents. That’s why pilots have a pitot heat system and check for blockage during preflight. It’s a classic failure mode in aviation, and it’s why the instrument has backup static sources and alternate airspeed references. A blocked pitot tube is a real hazard, not just a minor annoyance. But you know, it’s a bit like a tiny, stubborn goblin sitting on the tube’s mouth—if he won’t let the air in, the whole reading goes haywire. Though I’ll keep the goblin talk to a minimum from here on.",
